Woo, after 2-3 years of on/off playing, I finally did it...

I didn't take any notes for this game, as I really didn't have any faith in
my ascending until a couple of lucky breaks made me think otherwise, so this
is a fairly hazy recollection of the events that transpired - although this
was fairly quick in real-time terms (2-3 days), it's late (at the time of
posting this), and my memory isn't what it used to be :)

Cyriptix was the latest in a *very* long line of noble British valkyries
(Does that work? We should probably qualify for the cold resistance
intrinsic...) that I've been playing, mixed with the odd (very brief) visit
into the Dungeons with a Wizard - as I'm a bit of a packrat, and she started
with a strength stat of 16, I didn't think this would be going very far,
until a chance engraving with a long wand provided me with a lovely
kick-start to my game - this came along at about level 10 or so, certainly
before Sokoban, at any rate.

To the best of my recollection (using the wand from the castle too), I
wished for:

Blessed fixed +2 gray dragon scale mail
Blessed fixed +2 gauntlets of power
Blessed fixed +2 helm of telepathy
Blessed fixed +2 boots of speed
Blessed fixed +2 Hawaiian shirt
Blessed magic marker
Magic marker
2 blessed scrolls of charging
2 blessed scrolls of genocide
Another blessed scroll of genocide (Once I realized I should play safe, as
an ascension might just be on the cards)
Blessed fixed bag of holding

Reading that back, it seems like I didn't really earn any of my equipment in
a valiant fashion - perhaps my ascension is entirely due to the RNG smiling
on me for this one occasion... I think the only piece of armor I kept was my
cloak of protection.

I managed to keep Fluffy the cat alive for a lot longer than usual - he made
it all the way down to the dwarven mines and back again with me, where
another blessing, in the shape of a polymorph trap, changed him into a
silver dragon - keeping the name Fluffy amused me :) I nearly ditched him,
as it was taking *ages* to herd him to each and every downstair, but a wand
of haste monster, and later a magic whistle, eliminated that problem.

With my new bodyguard in tow, we trekked all the way back to DL2, and
systematically wiped out every shopkeeper in sight (sparing Izchak,
obviously), and repeated the following process a few times:

Priest:     Well hello, young adventurer - you'd like to donate money to our
cause, would you?
Me:        Indeed I would - here, please accept between 400 and 600 times my
experience level in gold.
Priest:    Why thank you, please accept this reduction in your armor class
in return.
Me:        You're too kind - there's someone I'd like you to meet, as it
goes... Fluffy? Here boy!
Priest:    Huh? Arggh!
(Various crunching and lip-smacking noises)

Unfortunately, Fluffy died a mysterious death at the hands of something
within the castle - by that point, after a careful perusal of the armour
spoiler (just to get my facts straight, you understand), I was at
roughly -25 AC, I didn't think I'd really miss him, so off I went, without a
moments remorse.

Reaching Medusa's island, I realized that nowhere in my travels had I come
across a way to cross water without meeting a moist end (if you'll pardon
the expression). So, another trek back to DL2 was required, to gather each
and every ring I'd left behind, and a brief polymorph session got me a shiny
ring of levitation. While I was there, I spent a fair bit of time doing some
inventory management, blessing and cursing scrolls, locking myself in tiny
rooms and enchanting things, until I got my final AC of -40, and was
wielding a +7 Excalibur, and a +6 silver saber. (Mjollnir and Sunsword were
obtained at some point, but due to their extra weight, I couldn't be arsed
to carry them around with me, and promptly forgot where I stashed them).

The quest level was uneventful, apart from the annoying circumstance of
/something/ within Lord Surtur's lair had managed to obtain a wand of
striking, so each time I approached a drawbridge leading in, I'd hear
*boing*, and then be confronted by a river of lava. What was even more
annoying, was the fact that this happened before my polymorph session, so I
had to come all the way back to circumvent this problem. (Yeah, this isn't
exactly a sequential account of events - but hey, I like it, it's got a Pulp
Fiction kind of feel to it)

Gehennom was just... boring, to tell the truth - lots of mapping levels, and
mindless hack 'n' slash (tm) action - with a silver saber acting as backup
to Excalibur, nothing really stood in my way for too long. Spent ages
looking for the entrance to Vlad's tower, before finding it under some
random gem, then quickly proceeded to nick the candelabrum, and head down to
the vibrating square.

At this point, I realized that I had exactly 0 candles to work with, so yet
*another* trip back to the main dungeon was required - I think there was
quite a lot of swearing and gesticulating on my part, at this point, but the
Orb of Fate made this relatively painless.

(The following snipped for the sake of brevity)

Dead Wizard, Book of the Dead, Lit Candelabrum, Rung Bell, Read Book, Dead
High Priest, Amulet in my Sweaty Little Grasp.

I was dreading facing Mr. Yendor, as I'd never actually gone up against him
before - but apart from popping up 5-6 times to say hello, and randomly
cursing a few lizard corpses (Not too bright, is he?), he didn't cause much
of a problem... I had 3 wands of death to greet him with, so I wasn't going
to sweat it too much anyhow.

Was flung back 3 times by the mysterious force - each time to the a level
that I hadn't fully mapped... I think this was to teach me a lesson, to be
honest, but I spit in the face of danger, and pressed on.

Made it all the way to DL1 without incident, dumped all unnecessary
equipment, and headed up to the plains - with a ring of conflict, and what I
assume is a reasonable armour class, I really didn't encounter too many
problems. No scrolls of magic mapping or gold detection though, so I spent
*far* too long digging tunnels, checking every non-lava space, floating
around in bubbles, etc.

Forgot to take my ring of conflict off before I hit the final level, though,
so probably made life a bit more difficult for myself at the last minute -
this was the only point at which my HP got below 200, I think I noticed when
I was at about 130 or so, quaffed some refreshing potions, and never got
below 200 again. Beat the snot out of Pestilence a few times, creamed Famine
without him ever laying a hand on me, and checked the altars. No.1 was...
someone elses, I forget who, but the second time was the charm, and Tyr's
shiny bauble was in his grasp.

